<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
  <parent>
    <groupId>com.codenameone.examples.dash</groupId>
    <artifactId>dash</artifactId>
    <version>1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
  </parent>
  <groupId>com.codenameone.examples.dash</groupId>
  <artifactId>dash-shared</artifactId>
  <version>1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>
  <name>dash-shared</name>

  <!--
    The REST contract and the objects it transfers, compiled once and used by
    both halves.

    The app (common) depends on this and its build generates the client: an
    implementation of each @RestClient interface and a mapper for each @Mapped
    class. The server (backend) depends on it too and its build generates the
    other half before compiling: an <Api>Server interface to implement and the
    controller that routes each operation to it. Change a signature here and
    whichever side did not follow stops compiling.

    Nothing is generated INTO this module, which is why it carries no Codename
    One plugin. It only has to compile, and for that it needs the annotations
    and the client's callback types, so the core is here at provided scope: the
    app brings its own, and the server must not receive it at all.

    Keep it to what both sides can run. No UI classes, and no JDK API the
    devices lack. The language is Java 17, the app's level, so that the
    transfer objects are records: both the app's generated mappers and the
    server's JSON codec build a record through its canonical constructor and
    read it through its accessors.

    The game itself lives here too, in com.codenameone.examples.dash.rules: one deterministic
    simulation that the app runs, the server re-runs as the authority and the
    replay viewer plays back. It must give the same answer to the bit on every
    runtime, so it uses no float or double, no clock, no java.util.Random and no
    String.split, and nothing of the core but plain Java. Its tests are ordinary
    JUnit 5 and run with `mvn -pl shared test`, with no server.
  -->
  <properties>
    <maven.compiler.source>17</maven.compiler.source>
    <maven.compiler.target>17</maven.compiler.target>
  </properties>

  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.codenameone</groupId>
      <artifactId>codenameone-core</artifactId>
      <scope>provided</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
      <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
      <version>5.9.3</version>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>3.0.0-M5</version>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
</project>
